name: the preprocessor
intro: Before the compiler sees your code a text pass runs: `#include`
intro: pastes a file in, `#define` replaces names with text, `#if` keeps
intro: or drops whole blocks. It works on tokens, not meaning -- it knows
intro: nothing of types or precedence. That is its power and its trap.
intro:
intro: Prefer a real feature when one exists. An integer constant is an
intro: `enum { MAX = 8 };`; `static const` is for typed values that need
intro: no constant expression. An `enum` is scoped and visible in a
intro: debugger. `#define MAX 8` is for what only the preprocessor can do.
intro:
intro: A function-like macro is substitution, so parenthesise every
intro: argument and the whole body:
intro:
intro:     #define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))
intro:
intro: Without them SQUARE(1 + 2) is 1 + 2 * 1 + 2, which is 5. An
intro: argument with a side effect runs once per appearance: SQUARE(i++)
intro: is undefined behaviour and MAX(f(), 0) calls f twice when f() is the larger. A
intro: `static inline` function has none of these problems; use one.
intro:
intro: A macro is one line; end a line with `\` to continue it.
intro: A macro of several statements goes in `do { ... } while (0)`: ONE
intro: statement, so it survives an unbraced `if`/`else`.
intro: `#x` turns an argument into a string literal; `a##b` glues two
intro: tokens into one name:
intro:
intro:     #define SHOW(e) printf("%s = %d\n", #e, (e))
intro:     #define GET(T) T get_##T(void) { return T##_val; }
intro:
intro: `__FILE__` and `__LINE__` are the file and line where they are
intro: written. Inside a macro they report the CALLER: how `assert` says
intro: where it failed. An X-macro writes a list once and expands it twice:
intro:
intro:     #define COLORS(X) X(RED) X(GREEN) X(BLUE)
intro:     #define AS_ENUM(c) COLOR_##c,
intro:     #define AS_NAME(c) #c,
intro:     enum { COLORS(AS_ENUM) COLOR_N };
intro:     static const char *name[] = { COLORS(AS_NAME) };
intro:
intro: Add a colour in one place and enum and names cannot drift apart.
intro: `#ifdef X`, `#ifndef X`, `#if EXPR`, `#else`, `#endif` choose code
intro: at compile time (`#ifdef NDEBUG` is how `assert` switches off);
intro: `#error "msg"` stops the build. Real programs are many `.c` files
intro: plus headers of declarations, each opened with an include guard
intro: (`#ifndef STACK_H` / `#define STACK_H` ... `#endif`) so a second
intro: include pastes nothing. Here every program is one file.
---
q: macrolab.c -- make the macros safe
spec: The file is given: `main` uses four macros, and the file starts them
spec: off as naive one-liners that compile and are wrong. Rewrite the
spec: macros and leave `main` alone. It must print exactly these lines and
spec: exit 0:
spec:
spec: `9`, `4`, `3`, `14`, `5 2`, `5 1`, `1 2`
spec:
spec: `SQUARE(x)` is x times x. `ABS(x)` is the absolute value. `MAX(a, b)`
spec: is the larger one, the plain macro: it names its winner twice, so
spec: the `5 2` line shows the winner's side effect running TWICE, while
spec: `5 1` shows it running once when the other one wins. `SWAP_INT(a, b)`
spec: swaps two ints and must be a single statement, because `main` uses
spec: it as the body of an `if` and under an `else`.
spec:
spec: Each of the first four lines goes wrong if an argument or the body
spec: is not parenthesised: `SQUARE(1 + 2)` must be 9, not 5.
file: macrolab.c
start: #include <stdio.h>
start: 
start: static int calls;
start: 
start: static int tick(int v)
start: {
start: 	calls++;
start: 	return v;
start: }
start: 
start: /* naive first tries: they compile, and they are wrong */
start: #define SQUARE(x) x * x
start: #define ABS(x) x < 0 ? -x : x
start: #define MAX(a, b) a > b ? a : b
start: #define SWAP_INT(a, b) ((void)0)    /* does nothing yet */
start: 
start: int main(void)
start: {
start: 	printf("%d\n", SQUARE(1 + 2));
start: 	printf("%d\n", 100 / SQUARE(2 + 3));
start: 	printf("%d\n", ABS(-5 + 2));
start: 	printf("%d\n", MAX(7, 3) * 2);
start: 
start: 	int m;
start: 	calls = 0;
start: 	m = MAX(tick(5), 1);
start: 	printf("%d %d\n", m, calls);
start: 	calls = 0;
start: 	m = MAX(tick(1), 5);
start: 	printf("%d %d\n", m, calls);
start: 
start: 	int a = 1, b = 2;
start: 	SWAP_INT(a, b);
start: 	if (a > b)
start: 		SWAP_INT(a, b);
start: 	else
start: 		a = 0;
start: 	printf("%d %d\n", a, b);
start: 	return 0;
start: }
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: static int calls;
prog: 
prog: static int tick(int v)
prog: {
prog: 	calls++;
prog: 	return v;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: #define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))
prog: #define ABS(x) ((x) < 0 ? -(x) : (x))
prog: #define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))
prog: #define SWAP_INT(a, b)         \
prog: 	do {                       \
prog: 		int t_ = (a);          \
prog: 		(a) = (b);             \
prog: 		(b) = t_;              \
prog: 	} while (0)
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	printf("%d\n", SQUARE(1 + 2));
prog: 	printf("%d\n", 100 / SQUARE(2 + 3));
prog: 	printf("%d\n", ABS(-5 + 2));
prog: 	printf("%d\n", MAX(7, 3) * 2);
prog: 
prog: 	int m;
prog: 	calls = 0;
prog: 	m = MAX(tick(5), 1);
prog: 	printf("%d %d\n", m, calls);
prog: 	calls = 0;
prog: 	m = MAX(tick(1), 5);
prog: 	printf("%d %d\n", m, calls);
prog: 
prog: 	int a = 1, b = 2;
prog: 	SWAP_INT(a, b);
prog: 	if (a > b)
prog: 		SWAP_INT(a, b);
prog: 	else
prog: 		a = 0;
prog: 	printf("%d %d\n", a, b);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: macrolab
case: macrolab; echo $?
case: macrolab | head -n 4
case: macrolab | tail -n 3
hint: wrap each use of the argument in parentheses, wrap the whole body too, and put the swap inside do with a while of zero
